/* Original interview companion notes. Source articles remain separately labelled. */
window.SD_GUIDES = {
 api:{name:'API design',diagram:['Client','API contract','Service','Data store'],
 basics:'An API is a contract: operations, input and output schemas, error behavior, and compatibility rules. Begin with the user’s workflow, then name resources and specify how clients recover from failure. REST is an architectural style, RPC expresses operations, GraphQL offers a typed query model, and WebSocket maintains bidirectional communication. These solve different interaction needs.\n\nFor a collection endpoint, define cursor pagination, maximum page size, filtering, ordering, and authorization for each object. GET is safe and idempotent in its intended semantics. PUT and DELETE are idempotent in effect, even when later responses differ. POST can support application-level idempotency through a persisted idempotency key. Idempotent never means that the returned data cannot change over time.',
 decisions:'Separate authentication from authorization. Validate the caller’s permission on every resource, not just at the gateway. Use timeouts, bounded request bodies, rate limits, versioning, and correlation identifiers. A status code must communicate what the client can do next: 400 for malformed input, 401 for missing or invalid authentication, 403 for refused access, 409 for a state conflict, and 429 for throttling.\n\nCompare protocols with your actual payloads and clients. Binary encoding does not guarantee a fixed speedup. GraphQL reduces some round trips but introduces query cost control and authorization work. A gateway centralizes cross-cutting entry behavior; it does not replace domain rules inside a service.',
 scenario:'Design an order-creation endpoint that a mobile client retries after a timeout.',model:'Accept a client-generated idempotency key scoped to the authenticated customer. Atomically associate that key with the request fingerprint and the committed order. A retry with the same payload returns the recorded result; reusing the key with another payload is rejected. Persist the result long enough for the retry window. Return an explicit pending state if completion is asynchronous, and define authorization, validation, error codes, and observability.',
 terms:[['Idempotency','Repeating an operation has the same intended effect as applying it once; response bytes need not be identical.'],['API contract','The documented operations, data shapes, permissions, errors, and compatibility guarantees clients depend on.']],
 quiz:[['A client times out after creating an order. Which prevents a retry from creating a duplicate?', ['Retry with a new random key every time','Persist a scoped idempotency key with the operation result','Assume every timeout means no work happened','Only increase the connection timeout'],1,'The server may have committed before the response was lost. A persisted key connects retries to the same logical operation.'],['What does idempotent mean?',['Every response is byte-for-byte identical','The intended effect of repeated identical operations matches one application','The operation never fails','No authorization is needed'],1,'Idempotency describes intended effect, not unchanging response data or guaranteed success.']]},
 identity:{name:'Identity and authorization',diagram:['User / client','Identity provider','Access token','Resource server'],
 basics:'Identification states who you are; authentication verifies that claim; authorization decides what you may do. A cookie is a browser storage and transport mechanism, a session is server-maintained login state, and a token is a credential representation. A session identifier can travel in a cookie. JWT is a token format, not a complete login protocol.\n\nOAuth delegates limited access to a resource. OpenID Connect adds an identity layer for authentication. For interactive public clients, authorization code with PKCE protects the code exchange. Client credentials represents a confidential application acting for itself, not a human user. An access token is presented to the intended resource server; an ID token has a different purpose.',
 decisions:'Use TLS and validate token issuer, audience, expiry, and allowed signing algorithms. A signed JWT’s payload is normally readable, so a signature must not be mistaken for encryption. Limit lifetime and scope; plan refresh-token rotation, logout, revocation, and key rotation. Browser session cookies should have suitable Secure, HttpOnly, and SameSite settings, with CSRF defenses where required.\n\nThe archive’s OAuth flow labels and some token descriptions are inaccurate. Client credentials is not a single-page-app login flow. Prefer current code-flow guidance rather than the implicit or resource-owner-password grants. Stateless validation does not remove the need for account disablement or revocation decisions.',
 scenario:'Design sign-in for a browser app that calls a separate API.',model:'Use an established OpenID Connect provider and authorization code with PKCE. Bind the callback to the initiating login, validate tokens and redirect destinations, and keep browser credential exposure small, for example through a backend-for-frontend session. The API checks access-token audience and object-level permissions. Define session expiry, logout, CSRF protections, key rotation, and account revocation behavior.',
 terms:[['OAuth','A framework for delegated authorization; it does not by itself define user authentication.'],['PKCE','A proof-key mechanism binding the authorization-code exchange to the initiating client.']],
 quiz:[['What is the client credentials grant intended for?',['A public browser app impersonating a user','A confidential client acting on its own behalf','Sending a user password to every service','Replacing TLS'],1,'Client credentials represents application authority. Interactive user authorization is a different flow.'],['What protection does a signed, unencrypted JWT provide?',['Payload secrecy','Integrity and verifiable authenticity when validated correctly','Automatic logout across every system','Protection from every replay'],1,'Signing detects tampering and authenticates the signer under the trust configuration. It does not hide payload contents or stop replay by itself.']]},
 security:{name:'Security boundaries',diagram:['Untrusted client','Validation / TLS','Authorization','Protected data'],
 basics:'Start with a threat model: what assets matter, who could misuse them, where trust boundaries lie, and what happens if one component is compromised. Encryption in transit protects network communication; encryption at rest protects stored data under defined key assumptions. Neither replaces authorization.\n\nPasswords should be verified with a suitable salted password-hashing function rather than reversibly encrypted or fast-hashed. A password manager has a different job: it must recover stored secrets, so its encrypted vault and key derivation are central. Do not copy a vendor-specific vault design as a universal standard.',
 decisions:'Use least privilege, secret management, input validation, bounded resource usage, audit logs, and key rotation. TLS authenticates the server using certificate validation and protects the channel using negotiated keys. Modern handshakes should not be reduced to the claim that the server private key simply decrypts every session key.\n\nTOTP depends on a shared secret and time window. Protect enrollment secrets and recovery paths, limit attempts, and account for clock drift. A second factor can still be phished. Firewalls filter traffic at specific layers; allowed traffic can still carry an application-level attack.',
 scenario:'A database containing user account records is stolen. What should limit the damage?',model:'Store password verifiers using a dedicated, appropriately tuned password-hashing scheme with unique salts, never plaintext. Keep unrelated encryption keys outside the database’s trust boundary, restrict production access, minimize retained sensitive data, and log administrative access. Plan credential reset and incident response. Explain the residual risk: offline guessing remains possible and depends on password strength and verifier cost.',
 terms:[['Threat model','An explicit account of assets, adversaries, trust boundaries, attack paths, and mitigations.'],['Salt','A unique value used in password hashing so identical passwords do not share the same stored verifier.']],
 quiz:[['Why are passwords not usually stored with reversible encryption?',['Encryption is always slow','Verification should not require recovering the original password','A salt is a secret master password','TLS already stores passwords safely'],1,'A dedicated password verifier reduces the need for recoverable secrets. Use suitable password hashing and unique salts.'],['Does a firewall make object-level authorization unnecessary?',['Yes','No, allowed requests still need application permission checks','Only for JSON','Only when using HTTPS'],1,'Network access and application authorization are separate controls with different trust boundaries.']]},
 network:{name:'Networks and protocols',diagram:['Application','Transport','IP routing','Peer application'],
 basics:'Trace a request through name resolution, connection establishment, secure transport, application bytes, and the response. TCP is a reliable ordered byte stream. UDP supplies datagrams without TCP’s delivery guarantees; protocols built on UDP can implement reliability themselves. HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP, while HTTP/2 typically uses TCP.\n\nThe OSI model is a conceptual seven-layer reference, not a literal list of seven processes every request visits. Encapsulation adds headers as data moves through a network stack; the receiver interprets and removes them. IP addresses route between networks; link-layer addresses identify interfaces on a local link.',
 decisions:'A DNS change is not an immediate global traffic switch: resolvers and clients cache answers. Routing policy can select endpoints by weight, geography, latency, or health, but it does not replace service-side balancing. Plan connection reuse, DNS TTL, timeout budgets, retransmission, and failover behavior.\n\nDistinguish bandwidth from latency and goodput. Increasing bandwidth does not remove propagation delay. URI syntax is scheme:[//authority]path[?query][#fragment]; a port follows the host with a colon, and a fragment is not ordinarily sent in an HTTP request. Never put secrets in URLs that may be logged.',
 scenario:'Walk through a browser’s first HTTPS request to a new hostname.',model:'Resolve the hostname using caches and DNS as needed. Establish the selected transport, negotiate TLS and validate the server certificate, then send the HTTP request. A reverse proxy or load balancer routes it to a healthy service, which may access caches and storage. Return the response, apply cache rules, and measure DNS, connect, TLS, server, and transfer time separately. Explain connection reuse and the HTTP/3 variant.',
 terms:[['Latency','Time taken for an operation or communication step, distinct from the rate of data transfer.'],['DNS TTL','A caching lifetime influencing how quickly a DNS answer can be refreshed.']],
 quiz:[['Why can a DNS failover take time to reach all clients?',['DNS always opens a new TCP session','Resolvers and clients may retain cached answers','A load balancer forbids failover','All TTLs are zero'],1,'Cached answers may remain in use until refreshed. Existing connections and client behavior can also delay movement.'],['Which is true about HTTP/3?',['It is raw unreliable HTTP with no recovery','It uses QUIC, which runs over UDP','It requires HTTP/2 over TCP underneath','It eliminates all network latency'],1,'QUIC supplies transport features over UDP. Using UDP does not mean the application simply gives up reliable delivery.']]},
 database:{name:'Database decisions',diagram:['Access pattern','Index / partition','Transaction','Durable storage'],
 basics:'Choose a database from access patterns: reads and writes per second, record size, query shape, transaction boundaries, consistency, retention, and recovery goals. Relational, document, key-value, wide-column, graph, and analytical systems make different trade-offs. A product name does not define all of its guarantees.\n\nACID atomicity gives all-or-nothing transactions; consistency preserves declared invariants; isolation controls concurrent interference; durability concerns committed state surviving failures under stated guarantees. Isolation levels differ. ACID consistency is different from the consistency discussed in CAP.',
 decisions:'An index accelerates selected lookups at the cost of write work and storage. Composite index order should reflect predicates and ordering. Use query plans and measured cardinality rather than assuming an index fixes every query. Sharding distributes records; replication copies them. Both require operational planning.\n\nFor sharding, choose a key that spreads work and supports your common queries. A hot tenant, celebrity, or time bucket can defeat even row distribution. Define cross-shard operations, rebalancing, replica lag, backup validation, and schema migrations. CAP concerns consistency versus availability during a partition; it is not an unrestricted pick-any-two slogan.',
 scenario:'Design message storage queried by channel and recent time.',model:'Partition primarily around channel access, with time buckets to bound partitions. Order messages with an appropriate identifier and use cursor pagination. Identify very hot channels, fan-out amplification, and retention. Choose an engine using measured read/write patterns, transaction needs, and operational expertise. Plan replication, recovery testing, and migration with dual-read or validation stages instead of treating a database switch as a magic scaling fix.',
 terms:[['Sharding','Splitting data across partitions, usually with a routing key.'],['Replication','Maintaining copies of data for availability, recovery, or read scaling.']],
 quiz:[['Does sharding mean the same thing as replication?',['Yes','No, sharding divides data and replication copies it','Only in SQL','Only for backups'],1,'They solve different problems and are often combined. Shard layout and replication strategy both affect failure behavior.'],['What does ACID consistency describe?',['Every read globally sees the newest write','Preservation of declared database invariants by valid transactions','Every replica is always reachable','No indexes are needed'],1,'ACID consistency concerns invariants. CAP consistency is a different distributed-system property.']]},
 cache:{name:'Caching',diagram:['Request','Cache lookup','Origin store','Cache fill'],
 basics:'A cache stores a reusable result close to the consumer. Choose the cached unit, key, lifetime, invalidation rule, and source of truth. Browser, CDN, proxy, application, and database caches sit at different layers. A hit improves the path only if lookup and validation cost less than recomputation or origin access.\n\nCache-aside loads missing values from the source and then fills the cache. Read-through puts loading behind the cache interface. Write-through updates the cache and durable path synchronously. Write-behind defers persistence, which creates a loss window. Write-around bypasses cache writes and relies on later reads to populate it.',
 decisions:'Define acceptable staleness and what happens if the cache disappears. A stale balance and a stale product description carry different risks. Use TTL jitter, request coalescing, prewarming, and bounded fallback concurrency to avoid a stampede. Negative caching can reduce repeated misses but must not conceal newly created objects for too long.\n\nEviction is not invalidation: capacity policy and correctness policy differ. For Redis persistence, compare RDB snapshots and AOF durability settings against recovery point and recovery time needs. Forking, copying, storage, and fsync can still affect latency; persistence is not universally free or nonblocking.',
 scenario:'A popular key expires and thousands of clients request it together.',model:'Coalesce concurrent fills for the same key, add TTL jitter, and permit bounded stale serving where the product allows it. Limit origin concurrency and use backpressure so cache failure does not become a database outage. Measure hit ratio together with origin load and tail latency. Define race-safe invalidation around writes and make the freshness requirement explicit.',
 terms:[['Cache stampede','Many requests simultaneously regenerate a missing or expired cached value, overloading the origin.'],['TTL','A time-to-live controlling how long a cached entry is eligible to remain fresh.']],
 quiz:[['What risk does write-behind introduce?',['All writes become instantly durable','Acknowledged updates can be lost before asynchronous persistence','No cache entries can expire','It removes the source of truth'],1,'Deferred persistence creates a durability window. Decide whether that loss is acceptable and how failures are recovered.'],['Which helps prevent a cache stampede?',['Identical expiry for every key','Request coalescing and bounded origin concurrency','Unlimited retries on every miss','Removing all timeouts'],1,'Coalescing reduces duplicate work, while concurrency limits protect the origin. Jitter can spread expirations.']]},
 messaging:{name:'Queues and event streams',diagram:['Producer','Durable broker','Consumer','External effect'],
 basics:'A broker decouples producers from consumers in time and rate. A work queue distributes tasks; a retained event log supports consumers that track their own position and can replay. Topics, partitions, consumer groups, and retention must be understood separately. Kafka ordering is within a partition, not a universal order across every message.\n\nAt-most-once processing can lose work. At-least-once processing can repeat work. Exactly-once claims have a boundary: transactions inside a streaming system do not automatically make an external payment or email happen exactly once. Match guarantees to the complete end-to-end workflow.',
 decisions:'Make consumers idempotent, define retry limits and a dead-letter process, and monitor lag plus processing age. Acknowledging before a durable side effect can lose work; acknowledging afterward can repeat it if the consumer crashes in between. An outbox ties an application update to an event record in one transaction.\n\nA queue absorbs a burst but cannot fix permanently insufficient consumer capacity. Bound backlog by age and storage, scale consumers within partition and ordering limits, and apply backpressure or admission control. Replay requires schema compatibility and care around side effects. Use batching and sequential I/O where appropriate rather than assuming one product is always fastest.',
 scenario:'An order service must update its database and publish OrderCreated without losing the event.',model:'Commit the order and an outbox entry atomically in the same database transaction. A relay publishes the entry and retries if necessary. Since the relay can publish twice around a crash, consumers deduplicate with a stable event ID or enforce an idempotent state transition. Track lag and dead letters, version the event schema, and reconcile missing downstream effects.',
 terms:[['Outbox pattern','Atomically persist a state update and an event-to-publish, then relay the event asynchronously.'],['Consumer lag','The gap between available log positions and a consumer’s progress; age also matters.']],
 quiz:[['A consumer commits its side effect and crashes before acknowledging. What can occur?',['The message may be redelivered','The broker proves no duplicate is possible','The database automatically rolls back a committed write','Ordering becomes global'],0,'Redelivery after an ambiguous acknowledgement is why consumers need idempotency.'],['What does adding a queue guarantee?',['Permanent overload disappears','Producers and consumers can be decoupled, within finite capacity','Exactly one external side effect always occurs','Every event is globally ordered'],1,'A queue buffers and decouples, but its storage and consumer throughput remain finite.']]},
 containers:{name:'Containers and orchestration',diagram:['Image registry','Deployment','Pods / processes','Service endpoint'],
 basics:'An image packages filesystem layers and configuration; a container is a running isolated process environment created from an image. Containers commonly share the host kernel rather than booting a full guest OS. Namespaces isolate views of resources and cgroups govern resource use. Packaging does not automatically remove security or compatibility concerns.\n\nKubernetes reconciles declared desired state with actual state. A Pod is a scheduling unit containing one or more containers. Deployments manage replica rollout, and Services provide stable discovery to changing Pod endpoints. Docker and Kubernetes are not interchangeable layers of the same problem.',
 decisions:'Set requests and limits from measurements. Readiness controls whether traffic is sent; liveness controls restart decisions; startup probes allow slow startup without premature restart. A healthy process can still be unable to serve useful traffic. Plan graceful shutdown, draining, configuration, secrets, and disruption budgets.\n\nClusterIP exposes an internal service endpoint; NodePort opens a node-level port; LoadBalancer requests an external load-balancing implementation; ExternalName returns a DNS alias. Stateful workloads still need persistent storage, backup, and recovery. Orchestration does not make databases stateless.',
 scenario:'Roll out a new API version without sending traffic to unready replicas.',model:'Use a rolling deployment with a compatible API and schema, a readiness probe that represents serving ability, and sufficient spare capacity. Start replacements before draining old replicas. Handle termination signals, stop accepting new traffic, and allow in-flight work to finish within a budget. Observe errors and latency, and define rollback behavior for both binaries and data changes.',
 terms:[['Readiness probe','A check used to decide whether an instance should receive service traffic.'],['Reconciliation','Continuously moving observed state toward declared desired state.']],
 quiz:[['Which probe should remove an unready Pod from service traffic?',['Readiness','Liveness only','Image name','A Git branch'],0,'Readiness gates traffic. Liveness can trigger a restart and should not be used as an indiscriminate dependency-failure detector.'],['Does a container normally boot its own independent kernel?',['Yes, always','No, containers commonly share the host kernel','Only for HTTP','Only with a registry'],1,'Containers differ from full virtual machines. Isolation, kernel compatibility, and resource controls still matter.']]},
 delivery:{name:'Delivery and release engineering',diagram:['Commit','Build / test','Artifact','Canary / rollout'],
 basics:'Continuous integration frequently merges and validates changes. Continuous delivery keeps changes releasable; continuous deployment automatically releases qualifying changes. A pipeline creates a traceable artifact from source, tests it, and promotes that artifact rather than rebuilding different bytes independently for each environment.\n\nTests serve different purposes: unit tests isolate behavior, integration tests check boundaries, contract tests protect interfaces, and end-to-end tests exercise workflows. Smoke, regression, performance, security, and resilience tests answer different risk questions. A large test count alone does not establish confidence.',
 decisions:'Use small changes, deterministic builds, signed or verified artifacts where appropriate, and protected release credentials. Canary or progressive delivery limits blast radius but only helps when signals detect regressions and rollback is understood. Feature flags separate deployment from release, while adding flag lifecycle and cleanup responsibilities.\n\nA binary rollback may not reverse a database migration or already emitted event. Use backward-compatible expand-and-contract changes and rehearse recovery. Build caches must include all relevant inputs in cache keys; a cache hit that hides a dependency change is a correctness bug.',
 scenario:'Deploy a breaking database field rename while old and new application versions coexist.',model:'First expand the schema to support both forms, deploy compatible readers and writers, migrate and verify data, then remove old usage only after no supported version depends on it. Define rollback and observability at each stage. Run representative integration and contract tests. Do not combine an irreversible schema deletion with a canary binary deployment and assume rollback is safe.',
 terms:[['Canary release','Expose a small portion of traffic or users to a change and evaluate it before broader rollout.'],['Expand and contract','Introduce compatibility, migrate use and data, then remove the obsolete interface.']],
 quiz:[['Why promote one tested artifact through environments?',['It avoids all testing','It preserves the identity of the bytes that were validated','It prevents every configuration issue','It removes dependencies'],1,'Rebuilding can introduce differences. Artifact identity improves traceability, while configuration still requires validation.'],['Can rolling back a binary always undo a schema migration?',['Yes','No, data changes may be incompatible or irreversible','Only when tests are unit tests','Only in Kubernetes'],1,'Release design must consider data and event compatibility, not only executable versions.']]},
 architecture:{name:'Architecture and boundaries',diagram:['User workflow','Domain boundary','Owned state','Integration'],
 basics:'An architecture allocates responsibilities and defines how components interact under constraints. Start with a small set of measurable quality goals: latency, availability, consistency, changeability, security, cost, and recovery. A modular monolith can have strong boundaries without network calls between every module.\n\nMicroservices support independent deployment and ownership, but add partial failure, distributed tracing, compatibility, and data coordination. Event-driven architecture changes temporal coupling; layered, hexagonal, and clean architecture organize dependencies. These ideas can coexist rather than forming mutually exclusive product choices.',
 decisions:'Define business invariants and transactional ownership before splitting services. A separate database per service is a logical ownership principle, not a reason to prohibit all shared infrastructure. A synchronous dependency chain multiplies latency and failure exposure. Asynchronous integration creates eventual-consistency and reconciliation work.\n\nDo not reproduce a famous company’s topology without its workload, organization, and operational history. Choose the simplest design meeting stated goals, then identify the measurable trigger for its next evolution. Cloud-native does not mean every application must be decomposed into dozens of services.',
 scenario:'A small team wants microservices because a successful company uses them.',model:'Clarify deployment contention, ownership boundaries, load characteristics, and failure-isolation needs. Begin with well-separated modules if those meet the requirements more cheaply. Extract one bounded capability only when independent scaling or change justifies it. Explain the added operational burden, data ownership, interface compatibility, observability, and failure handling, and define success metrics for the change.',
 terms:[['Bounded context','A boundary within which a domain model and its terminology have a consistent meaning.'],['Coupling','The degree to which one component’s behavior or changes depend on another.']],
 quiz:[['What is a sound reason to extract a service?',['Microservices are always modern','Independent ownership or scaling needs justify the operational cost','Every class needs a network endpoint','A diagram looks more complex'],1,'Architecture should follow workload and organizational needs, not fashion.'],['Does cloud-native automatically require microservices?',['Yes','No, cloud operating principles can also support modular monoliths','Only for small teams','Only for payment apps'],1,'Automation, resilience, and observability are not exclusive to microservices.']]},
 linux:{name:'Linux and operating systems',diagram:['Program / request','Process / kernel','Memory / scheduler','Device / storage'],
 basics:'A program is stored instructions; a process is a running instance with resources and an address space; threads are execution contexts within a process. Threads share many process resources but have their own stacks and execution state. Concurrency is overlapping progress, while parallelism means simultaneous execution.\n\nBoot proceeds through firmware, a bootloader, the kernel and early userspace, then the system’s init and service manager. Virtual memory maps process addresses to physical memory and backing storage. Filesystem paths, file descriptors, permissions, and device interfaces are related abstractions, not interchangeable objects.',
 decisions:'Diagnose before changing limits. For high CPU, inspect utilization, runnable work, and stack samples. For memory, distinguish resident use, cache, leaks, and allocation pressure. For I/O, inspect throughput, latency, queueing, and errors. For network issues, inspect connections, drops, and retransmissions. A single percentage rarely identifies the cause.\n\nLinux permissions apply to owner, group, and others. Directory execute means traversal; directory write controls entry changes. Signals notify, pipes stream bytes, sockets communicate locally or remotely, message queues deliver units, and shared memory requires synchronization. Commands should be understood before use, especially destructive ones.',
 scenario:'A service becomes slow while overall CPU looks moderate. What do you investigate?',model:'Check per-core saturation, run queues, I/O waits, memory pressure, garbage collection, locks, dependency latency, and connection pools. Compare with a baseline and correlate symptoms by time. Use process-level and system-level tools together, then profile the suspected bottleneck. Reproduce under a controlled workload rather than randomly increasing resources.',
 terms:[['Process','A running program instance with an address space and operating-system-managed resources.'],['Context switch','Changing the execution context running on a CPU, with costs that depend on the workload and system.']],
 quiz:[['What do threads within a process usually share?',['One execution stack','The process address space and many resources','No resources at all','A separate machine each'],1,'Threads share process resources while retaining their own execution state and stacks.'],['What does execute permission on a directory mainly allow?',['Editing every file body','Traversing/searching the directory','Deleting the disk','Running every file in it'],1,'Directory permissions have meanings different from regular-file permissions. Traversal is required to access entries through a directory.']]},
 git:{name:'Version control and collaboration',diagram:['Working tree','Index','Local commits','Remote refs'],
 basics:'Git records content-addressed objects and references to commit history. The working tree contains checked-out files, the index stages the next snapshot, and commits form a directed acyclic graph. A branch is a movable reference, not a separate physical copy of the entire repository. GitHub hosts collaboration around Git but is not Git itself.\n\nadd stages content; commit records a snapshot locally; fetch updates knowledge of remote history; push publishes references under server rules. A local commit does not need a network connection. Understand which locations each command affects before using it.',
 decisions:'Merge preserves the fact that histories converged; rebase replays changes on another base and creates new commit identities; squash combines changes into a new commit. Rewriting shared history can disrupt collaborators. Choose a workflow based on release cadence, review needs, and ownership rather than one universal strategy.\n\nA monorepo shares a repository boundary and can support atomic cross-component changes, while requiring build, ownership, and tooling discipline. Multiple repositories can isolate teams but complicate dependency updates. Neither repository layout implies one deployment architecture.',
 scenario:'A feature branch is behind main and already used by another developer.',model:'Inspect the current graph, coordinate with the collaborator, and prefer a non-destructive integration path when shared history should remain stable. If rebasing is agreed, understand that new commit IDs are created and use a protected update mechanism rather than blindly overwriting others’ work. Run relevant checks after conflict resolution and explain the resulting history.',
 terms:[['Index','Git’s staging area representing the content planned for the next commit.'],['Rebase','Replaying changes on a new base, producing new commit identities.']],
 quiz:[['Does git commit normally publish to the remote?',['Yes','No, it records a local commit','Only for Java','Only on main'],1,'Publishing refs is normally done by push. Commit is primarily a local operation.'],['What happens to commit identity when changes are rebased?',['It is guaranteed unchanged','Replayed commits get new identities','All remote branches are deleted','Files cannot change'],1,'Commit identity includes parent relationships, so moving a change onto another parent creates a different commit.']]},
 observability:{name:'Observability and diagnosis',diagram:['User request','Trace spans','Metrics / logs','Alert / action'],
 basics:'Metrics summarize numerical behavior over time, logs record discrete events, and traces connect work across boundaries. They answer complementary questions. Start with user-visible symptoms and service objectives: request success, latency, freshness, and correctness. A dashboard existing is not the same as having an actionable alert.\n\nMeasure percentiles, not only averages. A trace can expose a slow dependency in one request; aggregate metrics establish whether it is widespread. Propagate correlation context across queues as well as synchronous calls. Record enough context to diagnose without leaking credentials or personal data.',
 decisions:'Define an SLI, an SLO, and an error-budget policy. An availability objective must state the valid request population and measurement window. Sample traces intentionally and control metric label cardinality; user IDs as unbounded labels can make the monitoring system itself expensive or unreliable.\n\nAlert on symptoms with a concrete response. Pair alerts with runbooks, ownership, and enough evidence to distinguish overload from dependency failure. Logs are not a durable business ledger. A monitoring outage and a product outage should not silently become indistinguishable.',
 scenario:'Users report intermittent slowness but average latency is healthy.',model:'Inspect p95 and p99 latency by endpoint, region, tenant, and dependency. Correlate slow traces with saturation, queueing, garbage collection, and retries. Check sample bias and missing traffic. Form a hypothesis, run a targeted measurement, and validate the fix against the same percentile and workload rather than relying on a better overall average.',
 terms:[['SLO','A target for a measurable service-level indicator over a defined window.'],['Tail latency','Latency experienced by the slower portion of requests, often described with high percentiles.']],
 quiz:[['Why can average latency hide a serious incident?',['Averages always exceed maximums','A small subset can be very slow while most requests remain fast','Percentiles cannot be measured','Logs replace latency'],1,'Tail behavior matters to affected users and can be hidden by a mean across a much larger healthy population.'],['Which metric label is risky at high scale?',['A bounded HTTP status class','An unbounded unique user ID','A small fixed region set','A small fixed service set'],1,'Unbounded cardinality creates a time series for many distinct values and can overwhelm monitoring systems.']]},
 algorithms:{name:'Algorithms in distributed design',diagram:['Workload / key','Algorithm','Distribution / lookup','Cost / failure'],
 basics:'Pick an algorithm by the operation it must make efficient. Consistent hashing reduces remapping when nodes change; it still needs virtual nodes, load measurement, and hotspot handling. A Bloom filter can rule out membership with no false negatives under ordinary correct use, but positive answers may be false positives. A trie supports prefix lookup; spatial indexes narrow geographic candidates.\n\nRate limiters model an explicit policy. A token bucket permits bounded bursts while enforcing a refill rate. Fixed windows have boundary bursts; sliding approaches trade precision for memory and computation. A consensus protocol agrees on ordered state under a stated fault model; it is not simply a hash function.',
 decisions:'State time and space complexity, then explain practical skew and failure behavior. Consistent hashing balances keys probabilistically, not necessarily request rates. A hot key can dominate one partition. A globally strict limiter needs coordination; local limiters are fast but allow aggregate overshoot.\n\nFor IDs, compare uniqueness scope, ordering, coordination, predictability, and index behavior. Time-based IDs need a clock strategy and worker identity discipline. UUID versions differ; do not generalize random-ID behavior to every UUID. Use realistic cardinality and failure assumptions.',
 scenario:'Design an API limit of 100 requests per second with a burst allowance of 200.',model:'Use a token bucket with refill rate 100 tokens/second and capacity 200, charging the defined cost per request. Update state atomically for the chosen key. Explain whether limits are per user, API key, or tenant, what happens on limiter-store failure, and whether a distributed deployment enforces one global budget or approximate local budgets. Return a useful throttle response.',
 terms:[['Token bucket','A limiter with a bounded token capacity and refill rate, allowing controlled bursts.'],['Bloom filter','A probabilistic membership structure with possible false positives, not ordinary false negatives.']],
 quiz:[['A Bloom filter says an item may be present. What should you do for an exact lookup?',['Assume it definitely exists','Check the authoritative structure','Delete the filter','Assume it is absent'],1,'A positive can be a false positive. A negative can avoid a lookup under the filter’s normal assumptions.'],['What does consistent hashing not guarantee by itself?',['Reduced key movement on membership changes','Equal request load despite hot keys','A key-to-node mapping','The need to consider hash distribution'],1,'Equal key counts do not imply equal traffic or work. Hot keys and heterogeneous nodes require further measures.']]},
 payments:{name:'Payment-system correctness',diagram:['Merchant / client','Payment service','Processor / rail','Ledger / reconcile'],
 basics:'A payment workflow crosses parties and can have asynchronous or ambiguous outcomes. Separate authorization, capture, settlement, refunds, and disputes for the payment rail in question. Card networks, banks, processors, and gateways have different responsibilities. Real-time messaging is not automatically the same as final settlement.\n\nRepresent money with currency-aware exact arithmetic, not an arbitrary binary floating-point balance. Maintain an auditable ledger of transitions, a stable business identifier, and an explicit state machine. Keep sensitive payment data outside unnecessary components and follow the applicable integration boundary.',
 decisions:'Timeout does not mean payment failure. Retrying a debit without idempotency can charge twice. Persist a scoped idempotency key, deduplicate webhooks, and reconcile internal records against provider evidence. Notifications can be duplicated, delayed, and out of order; transitions must handle this deliberately.\n\nUse bounded retries with backoff and jitter, circuit breaking where appropriate, and a pending state for unknown outcomes. A distributed transaction with an external processor is usually unavailable, so use durable workflows and reconciliation. Provider and rail-specific legal, settlement, and timing guarantees must be checked rather than inferred from the archive.',
 scenario:'The processor accepted a charge but your request timed out before receiving its response.',model:'Record the attempt as pending or unknown, retain the same idempotency key, and query or retry through the provider’s supported idempotent mechanism. Do not create a new independent charge. Process signed callbacks idempotently, reconcile against provider records, and move the ledger only through valid transitions. Give the customer a truthful pending response instead of announcing an unverified failure.',
 terms:[['Reconciliation','Comparing independent transaction records and resolving mismatches or missing outcomes.'],['Ambiguous outcome','A timeout or failure leaves the caller unable to know whether the remote effect committed.']],
 quiz:[['A payment request times out. What can you safely conclude?',['The charge definitely failed','The outcome may be unknown','The charge happened twice','The ledger should be deleted'],1,'The remote party may have committed before the response was lost. Idempotency and reconciliation address this ambiguity.'],['How should duplicate payment webhooks be handled?',['Apply every callback as a new payment','Deduplicate and enforce valid state transitions','Ignore all callbacks','Trust arrival order as business order'],1,'Stable event IDs and state-machine checks protect against duplicates and out-of-order notifications.']]},
 scaling:{name:'Capacity and scaling',diagram:['Incoming load','Load balancer','Service replicas','Shared bottleneck'],
 basics:'Translate product demand into engineering estimates. Average QPS equals requests per day divided by 86,400; then choose a justified peak multiplier. Multiply record count by encoded size and include replication, indexes, metadata, retention, and growth. Estimate both requests per second and bytes per second.\n\nLittle’s Law relates average in-flight work to throughput times average time in a stable system. It is a useful sanity check, not a guarantee about tail latency under overload. Horizontal scaling adds instances, while vertical scaling enlarges a machine. A shared database or serialized lock can remain the bottleneck.',
 decisions:'Identify the limiting resource: CPU, memory, connection count, disk IOPS, network, a hot partition, or an external service quota. Stateless frontends can scale independently when state is externalized appropriately. Load balancing needs health checks, draining, and an algorithm suitable for request cost and connection duration.\n\nKeep headroom and test failure capacity. Two replicas are not redundant if one cannot carry the required load after the other fails. Autoscaling responds with delay and can amplify dependency overload. Backpressure, admission control, and bounded work are often as important as adding servers.',
 scenario:'Estimate capacity for 10 million daily users making 20 requests per day.',model:'That is 200 million requests/day, about 2,315 average requests/second. At a stated 5x peak, plan around 11,575 requests/second before safety margin. If a replica sustainably handles 500 requests/second at the target latency, 24 replicas cover that simplified peak before failure headroom. Explain benchmark assumptions, cache effects, dependencies, and the capacity required after a failure.',
 terms:[['Throughput','The rate of successfully completed work, measured with units and a time interval.'],['Headroom','Capacity reserved above expected load for bursts, growth, and failures.']],
 quiz:[['A service receives 86,400,000 requests/day. What is average QPS?',['100','1,000','86,400','1,000,000'],1,'Divide requests/day by 86,400 seconds/day. Peak traffic and failure headroom require separate assumptions.'],['Why might adding API replicas fail to improve throughput?',['Horizontal scaling never works','A shared downstream database may already be saturated','Replicas always eliminate locks','More replicas reduce request count'],1,'Scaling a non-bottleneck does not remove the limiting resource and can increase downstream contention.']]},
 reliability:{name:'Failure and recovery',diagram:['Dependency call','Timeout budget','Retry / breaker','Recovery path'],
 basics:'Distributed systems fail partially: one component may time out while others are healthy. Define the availability objective and acceptable data loss and downtime. RPO is the tolerated loss window; RTO is the target time to restore service. Replication and backups address different failure modes.\n\nUse deadlines, bounded concurrency, retry budgets, circuit breakers, and bulkheads intentionally. Retries can help transient failures but amplify overload. A circuit breaker stops repeatedly calling a dependency that appears unhealthy; it requires a recovery probe policy and does not decide business correctness for you.',
 decisions:'Make retries safe with idempotency and exponential backoff plus jitter. Allocate an end-to-end latency budget across hops. If every layer retries independently, one request can multiply into many attempts. Prefer controlled retries at a defined layer.\n\nTest restores rather than only successful backup jobs. Spread replicas across independent failure domains and verify remaining capacity. A multi-region design adds replication latency, coordination, conflict handling, and operational cost. Name the concrete disaster being addressed before adding a second region.',
 scenario:'A dependency slows down and each of three layers retries three times.',model:'Show how retry multiplication can overwhelm the struggling dependency. Introduce one retry owner, bounded attempts, backoff with jitter, deadline propagation, and concurrency limits. Classify retryable errors and require idempotency for effects. Measure dependency latency and success, shed low-priority work where valid, and probe recovery without unleashing the whole backlog.',
 terms:[['RPO','Recovery point objective: the acceptable amount of data loss expressed as a time window.'],['RTO','Recovery time objective: the targeted time to restore an acceptable service level.']],
 quiz:[['What is a retry storm?',['Retries reduce traffic to zero','Multiple failed requests generate enough extra attempts to worsen overload','Every timeout is a success','A backup rotation schedule'],1,'Retries are additional load and require budgets, safe semantics, and spacing.'],['Does a completed backup prove the service can be restored on time?',['Yes','No, restore correctness and duration must be tested','Only for SQL','Only for object stores'],1,'A recovery objective needs evidence from restoration, not only backup creation.']]},
 storage:{name:'Storage and data processing',diagram:['Incoming data','Object / log store','Processing','Query / serve'],
 basics:'Object storage addresses objects by a key and stores metadata with payload bytes. It suits media, archives, backups, and data lakes. Block storage exposes blocks to a filesystem or database, while file storage exposes a directory-oriented interface. The access model and update behavior matter more than just capacity.\n\nA warehouse typically serves curated analytical models; a lake retains a wider set of data forms; lakehouse approaches combine capabilities. Batch processing operates on bounded input. Streaming handles continuing input and must define windows, late events, ordering, state, and replay.',
 decisions:'Estimate storage cost together with access, transfer, replication, retrieval, and retention policies. Design object keys and partitioning around lookup patterns. Versioning and lifecycle rules can help but do not replace deletion semantics, access control, or tested recovery.\n\nIn a stream pipeline, distinguish event time from processing time. A watermark expresses progress assumptions; it is not proof that no older event will ever arrive. Define late-data handling and idempotent sinks. Long-term backfills can overload production serving paths unless isolated and throttled.',
 scenario:'Design a user-video upload and playback pipeline.',model:'Issue a short-lived scoped upload capability, upload directly to object storage using resumable or multipart transfer, and validate completion. Persist metadata, publish a durable transcoding job, and create multiple renditions asynchronously. Serve manifests and segments through a CDN with access controls. Handle duplicate jobs, failed transcoding, moderation, lifecycle policy, and the difference between upload completion and playback readiness.',
 terms:[['Event time','When an event occurred according to its data, distinct from when a processor handles it.'],['Object storage','A storage abstraction for keyed objects and metadata, useful for large immutable or replaced payloads.']],
 quiz:[['Which is essential for event-time streaming?',['Assume every event arrives on time','Define late-data and watermark handling','Never keep state','Use only a relational table'],1,'Arrival order and event time can differ. The system must state its lateness policy.'],['Should large video uploads always pass through an application server’s memory?',['Yes','No, direct scoped object uploads can avoid that bottleneck','Only if a CDN exists','Only if files are small'],1,'Direct upload with scoped credentials can remove an unnecessary bandwidth and memory bottleneck while preserving authorization.']]},
 patterns:{name:'Design patterns and data structures',diagram:['Problem','Invariant','Pattern / structure','Trade-off'],
 basics:'A pattern names a recurring design solution and its consequences. Factory centralizes creation, builder assembles a complex object, adapter translates an interface, decorator adds behavior, strategy swaps an algorithm, and observer distributes change notifications. Name the problem before selecting a pattern.\n\nData structures have operation-specific costs. Arrays offer indexed access, linked lists cheap local relinking, hash tables expected fast exact lookup, trees ordered traversal, heaps priority selection, and graphs relationship traversal. An in-memory structure and an on-disk index differ in cache, page, and I/O behavior.',
 decisions:'Compare B-tree-family indexes for ordered page-oriented access with LSM-based designs that accumulate writes and compact sorted runs. Compaction, write amplification, read amplification, and space amplification affect latency and cost. Bloom filters can avoid some unnecessary reads.\n\nPatterns do not eliminate ownership, concurrency, or lifecycle decisions. A singleton can hide shared mutable state; an observer chain can create ordering and failure surprises. Choose the smallest abstraction that clarifies the domain, then show how it changes when a requirement changes.',
 scenario:'Design a notification subsystem that can support email, SMS, and push providers.',model:'Define a provider interface and use strategies or adapters for channel-specific behavior. Keep policy such as preferences and priority separate from delivery transport. Persist work, enforce idempotency, bound retries, and record delivery state. Avoid a single giant conditional that mixes business rules with every vendor API, but also avoid unnecessary layers for a tiny fixed requirement.',
 terms:[['Strategy pattern','Encapsulate interchangeable algorithms behind a common interface.'],['Write amplification','The ratio between physical write work and the logical data the application writes.']],
 quiz:[['When is a strategy useful?',['Whenever a class exists','When an algorithm varies behind a stable interface','To avoid all interfaces','To make every operation global'],1,'Strategy separates the choice and implementation of an algorithm from the caller that uses it.'],['What is a typical cost of LSM compaction?',['Zero background I/O','Additional read/write work and potential latency interference','No space amplification','Automatic global ordering'],1,'Compaction reorganizes sorted data and consumes resources. Scheduling and workload shape matter.']]},
 languages:{name:'Execution models and runtimes',diagram:['Source code','Compiler / bytecode','Runtime / JIT','Machine execution'],
 basics:'A language specification and its implementation are different. C and C++ are commonly compiled ahead of time; JVM languages commonly use bytecode and runtime optimization. CPython compiles source to bytecode and interprets it, while many JavaScript engines use JIT compilation. The archive’s simple compiled-versus-interpreted split is an approximation.\n\nPerformance depends on workload, algorithm, allocation, I/O, runtime warmup, compiler settings, and hardware. A language’s benchmark ranking cannot establish that every application written in it is faster or more energy-efficient. Cross-platform source does not mean one native binary runs on every operating system.',
 decisions:'Match runtime properties to requirements: startup time, throughput, memory, concurrency support, native dependencies, and team skill. Managed memory can improve productivity while introducing collection behavior to understand. Native code still needs a disciplined ownership model.\n\nFor portable deployment, distinguish source portability, bytecode/runtime compatibility, ABI, architecture, operating-system calls, and packaged dependencies. Containers package environments but do not magically translate processor instruction sets or incompatible kernels.',
 scenario:'A team claims changing language will fix an API latency problem.',model:'Measure where latency is spent first: network, database, queueing, locks, runtime pauses, or computation. If CPU is dominant, profile representative requests and compare implementations with warmup, concurrency, and tail latency controlled. Include migration and operational cost. An algorithmic or query improvement may dominate a runtime switch.',
 terms:[['JIT compilation','Compiling code during execution, often using observed runtime behavior to optimize it.'],['ABI','The binary-level calling and layout conventions needed for compiled components to interoperate.']],
 quiz:[['Is Python universally executed without any compilation step?',['Yes','No, CPython ordinarily compiles to bytecode','Only on Linux','Only inside Docker'],1,'Interpreted is an implementation description that often includes bytecode compilation; the archive’s split is simplified.'],['What should precede a language migration justified by latency?',['Choosing a benchmark winner','Profiling the actual bottleneck','Adding more frameworks','Removing measurement'],1,'A runtime change will not fix a dominant database query, network delay, or algorithmic issue by itself.']]},
 ui:{name:'Client architecture',diagram:['User action','View / state','Application logic','Backend / storage'],
 basics:'Client patterns separate presentation, state, and behavior. MVC, MVP, and MVVM assign responsibilities differently, and platform conventions affect the exact meanings. The goal is testable behavior and predictable state changes, not memorizing diagram labels without understanding data flow.\n\nBrowsers also isolate work across processes and threads. Rendering involves resource loading, parsing, style and layout, painting, compositing, and script execution. A slow user interaction can involve main-thread work even when the API is fast.',
 decisions:'Define which state is local, shared, derived, or persisted. Keep expensive work out of interaction paths, handle request cancellation and stale responses, and design loading, empty, error, and offline behavior. Accessibility is part of the contract: keyboard operation, readable contrast, focus, and understandable feedback.\n\nDo not assume one tab equals exactly one operating-system process in every browser situation. Isolation models and sharing vary. Treat the archive’s browser diagram as a conceptual explanation rather than a fixed process-count rule.',
 scenario:'A search box displays results from an older query after a newer one.',model:'Track request identity or cancel obsolete work so only the latest relevant response updates state. Debounce where appropriate, cache safely, and display loading and errors without losing focus. Separate view state from network orchestration and test out-of-order responses, rapid typing, slow networks, and keyboard navigation.',
 terms:[['Derived state','A value computed from existing state rather than stored independently and synchronized manually.'],['Race condition','Behavior depends on timing between operations that should have a controlled relationship.']],
 quiz:[['How can stale search responses be prevented from replacing newer results?',['Assume requests finish in order','Use request identity or cancellation checks','Disable every network call','Increase font size'],1,'Response order need not match request order. Apply results only when they still correspond to the current intent.'],['Does a fast API guarantee a responsive interface?',['Yes','No, rendering and main-thread work can still block interaction','Only for mobile','Only without caching'],1,'Client-side processing and rendering are separate parts of end-to-end responsiveness.']]},
 career:{name:'Interview practice and technical judgment',diagram:['Requirement','Reasoned decision','Evidence','Reflection'],
 basics:'Interview preparation combines knowledge with clear reasoning under uncertainty. Start by clarifying the problem, define scope, explain assumptions, make a simple design, and explore its highest-risk trade-off. For coding, communicate invariants and complexity; for design, connect components to requirements; for behavioral questions, make your individual actions and results clear.\n\nReading a list of resources is not practice. Retrieve a concept without notes, implement or diagram it, compare alternatives, and ask a follow-up that changes one assumption. Maintain an error log so repeated weak areas determine the next practice session.',
 decisions:'For a behavioral answer, state the situation, task, action, and result, then explain what you learned. Use honest evidence, not invented scale or ownership. A good technical answer can admit missing information and propose how to obtain it.\n\nFor historical company case studies, distinguish the article’s claims from transferable principles. State the context and date, question unsupported generalizations, and avoid treating tool popularity as proof of fitness. Interview expectations vary by role and level; use the role description to prioritize practice.',
 scenario:'You do not recognize a technology the interviewer asks about.',model:'State what you know and where your knowledge ends. Ask what capability or constraint matters, then reason from familiar principles such as durability, access patterns, failure domains, and latency. Offer a provisional design with explicit assumptions and identify what documentation or experiment would validate it. Do not fabricate production experience.',
 terms:[['STAR','Situation, task, action, result: a structure for specific behavioral examples.'],['Active recall','Retrieving an idea before rereading it, then checking and correcting your explanation.']],
 quiz:[['What makes a design answer persuasive?',['Listing as many products as possible','Linking decisions and trade-offs to explicit requirements','Never asking a question','Claiming every design is optimal'],1,'Reasoned decisions, assumptions, and failure analysis are more useful than a technology shopping list.'],['What should you do when a historical stack diagram is used in an interview?',['Assert it is the current stack','Discuss the context and extract principles without treating it as current fact','Ignore every trade-off','Copy every component'],1,'Company systems evolve. Explain the engineering idea rather than presenting an archive as live architecture documentation.']]},
 ai:{name:'AI-system architecture',diagram:['Input / retrieval','Model inference','Validation / tools','Response / evaluation'],
 basics:'A language model predicts tokens conditioned on its input. Training learns parameters; inference uses those parameters to generate output. Pretraining, supervised tuning, and preference optimization are distinct stages, and exact methods vary. Historical diagrams of a product are not a complete or current specification of its internals.\n\nFor a system-design answer, separate the model from the surrounding application: data ingestion, indexing and retrieval, prompt construction, inference scheduling, tool execution, authorization, output validation, and evaluation. A plausible response is not proof of factual correctness.',
 decisions:'Measure task success, groundedness, latency, cost, and failure cases with a representative evaluation set. Retrieval can supply relevant context but does not guarantee faithful use. Treat retrieved documents and model outputs as untrusted data, particularly when tools can change external systems.\n\nServing considerations include token budgets, batching, caching, streaming, queueing, rate limits, and cancellation. Keep user data access scoped. The archive’s GPT-3.5/GPT-4-era product descriptions are historical and must not be used as current model capability or architecture guarantees.',
 scenario:'Design an assistant that answers questions over a company’s private documentation.',model:'Ingest and version documents, preserve access-control metadata, index chunks, and retrieve only documents the user can access. Compose a bounded prompt with citations, execute inference, and validate answer grounding through evaluations. Protect tool use and secrets from prompt injection, record suitable telemetry, and provide an abstention path when evidence is absent. Measure quality and cost with realistic tasks.',
 terms:[['Inference','Using a trained model to produce outputs from inputs.'],['Grounding','Connecting claims to relevant evidence, which must still be checked for faithful support.']],
 quiz:[['Does adding retrieval guarantee a correct model answer?',['Yes','No, retrieval and faithful use both need evaluation','Only for long prompts','Only for PDFs'],1,'The right evidence can be missing, irrelevant, or misused. Evaluate retrieval and answer support separately.'],['Can a 2023 product diagram establish current model internals?',['Yes','No, it is historical context with incomplete public details','Only if it includes arrows','Only if the product name is unchanged'],1,'Product behavior and implementations change. Historical diagrams cannot establish current internals.']]},
 experiments:{name:'Experimentation and recommendation',diagram:['Eligible user','Assignment / candidates','Treatment / ranking','Metrics / guardrails'],
 basics:'An experiment needs a hypothesis, an eligibility rule, a randomization unit, a stable assignment, and defined outcome and guardrail metrics. Recommendation pipelines often generate candidates, filter them, score and rank them, then enforce product or safety constraints. Offline model quality and online product impact are different measurements.\n\nChoose a randomization unit that matches interference and identity behavior. User-level assignment differs from request-level assignment. Sample-ratio mismatch, logging gaps, and exposure errors can invalidate a seemingly clean result.',
 decisions:'Define power and minimum detectable effect before reading the result. Avoid repeatedly peeking at an ordinary fixed-horizon test and treating each view as an independent decision. Consider novelty effects, seasonality, multiple comparisons, and spillover.\n\nFor recommendation serving, balance freshness, latency, diversity, and relevance. Explain candidate fan-out, feature retrieval, model serving, cache staleness, and graceful fallback. A historic number of candidates or model parameters is a case-study detail, not a universal sizing rule.',
 scenario:'Design a platform to compare two ranking algorithms safely.',model:'Assign eligible users deterministically to stable variants, log actual exposure with a versioned experiment ID, and collect outcome and guardrail events. Validate sample ratios and logging before interpreting effects. Set decision rules and rollout limits in advance. Provide a fallback ranking and a kill switch, and avoid letting control and treatment share mutable state that contaminates results.',
 terms:[['Guardrail metric','A metric that must remain acceptable even if the primary experiment objective improves.'],['Randomization unit','The entity assigned to a variant, chosen to match the experiment and interference assumptions.']],
 quiz:[['Why log actual exposure instead of only assignment?',['Assignments never matter','A user may be assigned but never receive the treatment','It eliminates every bias','It replaces randomization'],1,'Exposure records connect outcomes to what the user actually experienced and help detect delivery failures.'],['What is a risk of request-level randomization for a persistent UI change?',['Users can switch variants repeatedly','Every user stays in one variant','It guarantees no interference','It improves all retention metrics'],0,'The randomization unit should match the experience and the analysis. Stable user-level treatment is often required.']]},
 cost:{name:'Cost and performance trade-offs',diagram:['Workload','Compute / storage','Transfer / requests','Total cost'],
 basics:'Estimate total cost from the workload, not just one advertised unit price. Compute, storage, requests, data transfer, inter-zone traffic, logs, NAT, load balancing, backup, idle capacity, and staff effort can all matter. Prices and product limits in a historical archive are not current quotations.\n\nServerless shifts operational responsibility and billing granularity, but it still runs on servers and still requires capacity, security, and recovery thinking. Compare steady and bursty workloads, utilization, latency requirements, and operational skill rather than declaring one deployment style universally cheaper.',
 decisions:'Optimize measured dominant costs while preserving objectives. A cross-service media pipeline can spend more on orchestration and transfer than compute; combining stages may reduce those costs but reduce independent scaling. An object tier with cheap storage can have expensive retrieval or minimum-duration charges.\n\nUse a scenario model with units and sensitivity analysis. Change request rate, payload size, retention, replication, and transfer assumptions separately. Validate the current vendor rates before a financial decision; the course calculator uses illustrative inputs rather than a live quote.',
 scenario:'A media-processing pipeline is expensive. Should it be rewritten as a monolith?',model:'Break the bill down by compute, storage, orchestration, requests, and network transfer. Measure utilization and data movement between stages. Compare co-location or batching against scaling and failure-isolation needs. Prototype the bottleneck, preserve correctness and recovery behavior, and evaluate cost per processed minute under representative demand. Do not infer a general rule from one company’s reported percentage.',
 terms:[['Unit economics','Cost and value expressed per useful unit such as request, stored GB-month, or processed video minute.'],['Egress','Data leaving a defined network or service boundary, often with a distinct transfer charge.']],
 quiz:[['Why is storage price per GB insufficient for an architecture comparison?',['Requests and transfer are always free','Access, transfer, replication, retention, and operations also contribute','All storage performs identically','Capacity cannot be estimated'],1,'A complete cost model includes the workload’s access and lifecycle behavior, not only bytes retained.'],['What can a reported case-study cost reduction prove?',['Every similar service will save the same amount','The change helped under that case’s assumptions','All serverless systems are inefficient','Microservices never scale'],1,'A case study is evidence in context. Transfer the reasoning and validate your own workload.']]}
};
